<newsitem itemid="130125" id="root" date="1996-10-21" xml:lang="en">
<title>SOUTH AFRICA: Foodcorp yr shr, div jumps.</title>
<headline>Foodcorp yr shr, div jumps.</headline>
<dateline>JOHANNESBURG 1996-10-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year ended Aug 31</p>
<p>			(million rand unless stated)</p>
<p>    Shr, attrib (cts)     268.2	 vs 230.6</p>
<p>    Div (cts)		 112.0	 vs 27.0</p>
<p>    Attrib income	   144	   vs 123</p>
<p>    Pre-tax		   194	   vs 184</p>
<p>    Tax			 48	    vs 59</p>
<p>    Interest paid	   10	    vs 18</p>
<p>    Turnover		  3,238	 vs 3,088</p>
<p>Div payable Dec 6</p>
<p>on register Nov 15</p>
<p>Full name: Foodcorp Ltd</p>
<p>    Extraordinary profit  none	  vs 84</p>
<p>NOTE - Analysts polled by Reuters forecast fully diluted attributable year earnings at between 262 and 267 cents per share with a consensus of 264.7 cents. Forecasts on a dividend ranged from 80 to 121 cents.</p>
<p>Directors said the group was seen producing earnings growth of between 12 and 15 percent in the new financial year despite the strain exhibited by the economy, and the food sector in particular, which resulted in increased competititive pressures.</p>
<p>They said the group's strong brand positioning, reduced exposure to the volatile red meat cycle and low cost of production of its staple operations would help it achieve the forecast growth.</p>
<p>-- Johannesburg newsroom +27 11 482 1003</p>
</text>
